    based on a variable that is itself affected by treatment, or if the “overall”
    comparison is unadjusted and the group sizes differ so radically that the overall
    result is driven by a different mix of patients rather than by the treatment’s
    effect. But that is not a genuine case of being better in every subgroup yet worse
    overall; it is a case of comparing the wrong quantities.
