    association can reverse when groups are combined. However, Simpson’s paradox applies
    to observational associations, not to a properly defined causal treatment effect
    across the same subgroups. If the treatment truly is better in every subgroup, the
    correct overall conclusion must also favor the treatment when the subgroups are
    combined correctly. The apparent reversal comes from confounding or from ignoring
    how the groups are weighted, not from the treatment itself.
